                        "entry_id": "67335b47-d838-3c14-bd6b-df6a2e0e4cfc",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A toad (chiefly from the family Bufonidae)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Caribbean",
                            "Guyana",
                            "Trinidad-and-Tobago"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "75d376f2-d20d-418a-8589-858e7f0f18b9",
                                "sense_id": "8a5ed544-11c0-362c-871a-04d846eef367",
                                "text": "Medical attention is immediately needed if \"Fido\" encounters a specie of huge toad, up to eight to ten inches round, called a \"crappo\", and decides to take it into his mouth to play with. This toad exudes a substance from glands in its skin which will cause profuse salivation, and sometimes, if absorbed, will cause the animal to become very excited with high fever and twitching uncontrollable motions."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c071089e-c7cf-4768-a29d-93adad953000",
                                "sense_id": "8a5ed544-11c0-362c-871a-04d846eef367",
                                "text": "crapo (from the French crapaud, frog) a huge toad (Bufo marinus)"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c3b9cf44-e7e9-45cb-8a7f-2c7f32c61bbc",
                                "sense_id": "8a5ed544-11c0-362c-871a-04d846eef367",
                                "text": "One of the wild animals most familiar to all Trinidadians, even those with not the slightest interest in wildlife, is the crappo, that large toad which is seen so often at this time of year around houses and roads."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b0744580-3a89-4277-b11b-515df80b3a5e",
                                "sense_id": "8a5ed544-11c0-362c-871a-04d846eef367",
                                "text": "Little crappos would find their way into the house. I was tormented by them. When in panic I feared they, like fluttering pigeons, were liable to do anything."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0340f368-1ab4-4ab5-a7f8-d05f788a272a",
                                "sense_id": "8a5ed544-11c0-362c-871a-04d846eef367",
                                "text": "This sentry type wooden cubicle was built in the yard; not far from the house. It was not a very respectful bathroom in my opinion; especially since the fat crappos (crapaud/huge toad) took up residence. How I despised them."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f8e72c83-715c-4a50-9e11-4e385a5939cc",
                                "sense_id": "8a5ed544-11c0-362c-871a-04d846eef367",
                                "text": "Crickets call out in the coming dark. Crappo answer."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "aecd0e8a-c332-31ee-9b3d-46007211ac9d",
                "word": "crappo",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "71327077-0059-3156-bbf7-ee511993420c",
                        "entry_id": "aecd0e8a-c332-31ee-9b3d-46007211ac9d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The andiroba or crabwood tree (Carapa guianensis)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Trinidad-and-Tobago"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f9abfc85-77da-4b06-921a-dd3d0ccffa5c",
                                "sense_id": "71327077-0059-3156-bbf7-ee511993420c",
                                "text": "Carapa or Crappo […] Carapa Guianensis, Aubl."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "aa22440a-be8e-4c6d-84eb-51f50a6aeec6",
                                "sense_id": "71327077-0059-3156-bbf7-ee511993420c",
                                "text": "Crappo, or carapa (Carapa guianensis), 40 to 60 feet high and 1½ to 3 feet in diameter, grows plentifully in mixed forests. The reddish wood weighs 42 pounds per cubic foot, and is durable and easily worked."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8a89f96d-7e55-48fa-9bdd-07e9f43f825c",
                                "sense_id": "71327077-0059-3156-bbf7-ee511993420c",
                                "text": "Besides Cedar, there are only five kinds of timber that are sufficiently plentiful to allow of their export viz.: Crappo or Crabwood (Carapa guianensis), Mora (Dimorphandra Mora), Olivier (Chuncoa obovata) Guatacre or Watercaire (Lecythis laevifolia) and Fustic (Macura Xanthoxylon). Crappo or Crabwood somewhat resembing Mahogany—a useful furniture wood—and Fustic—a dyewood—are fairly well known."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "90c7f5ad-1356-4664-b695-70e33021a55c",
                                "sense_id": "71327077-0059-3156-bbf7-ee511993420c",
                                "text": "With the Forest Reserves there are now more than 1,500 acres of plantations (including experimental plots) of which the principal indigenous species are:— […] crappo (Carapa guianensis, Aubl.), […] ten acres were regenerated with crappo under contracts at the Central Range Reserve Plantations, whilst ten acres in the Southern Watershed Reserve were partly planted with teak and partly with indigenous species."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7ad8446d-d89c-4e30-aeed-cb197af306df",
                                "sense_id": "71327077-0059-3156-bbf7-ee511993420c",
                                "text": "The commonest large trees in the Bois Mulatre-Carat forest areas are crappo Carapa guianensis and wild chataigne Pachira insignis, with guatecare Eschweilera subglandulosa in third place among acurel Trichilia oblanceolata, rosemacho Crudia glaberrima and redwood Gaurea guara."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b5648452-e85d-4a4a-be83-4b9ff6913409",
                                "sense_id": "71327077-0059-3156-bbf7-ee511993420c",
                                "text": "[C]edar, crappo, olivier and mora dominate the harvest from state forests."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f677bbb0-f029-4e1b-a469-e165c003ecb1",
                                "sense_id": "71327077-0059-3156-bbf7-ee511993420c",
                                "text": "Mahogany (Swietenia macrophylla G. King and S. mahagoni Jacq.), the American cedar (Cedrela odorata L. (= C. mexicana Roem.)) and crappo (Carapa guianensis Aubl.) are forest trees of commercial value in the Caribbean region."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
